sometimes dolphins leap out of the water  for plain fun  other times they're twisting and turning  to shake off pesky hitchhikers known as  remoras  remoras save energy by sticking to all  sorts of surfaces from the hard shell of  turtles to the rough skin of sharks  while remore is burst out of the water  by attaching to other swimmers  some animals such as the kingfisher can  propel themselves into water and out of  it snagging prey along the way  inspired by the strong grip of  suckerfish and the swift swoop of  kingfishers researchers set out to  create the ultimate swimming flying and  retrieving robot  drones and survey bots can't lug giant  batteries on board they need to conserve  power whenever possible  the researchers set out to replicate the  remora's energy saving strategy by  recreating its sticky disk  remoras use lamellae slat like bones  encased in soft tissues and spinules  rows of hard teeth like tissue to latch  on  in 2017 the researchers created a  layered disk inspired by these features  but two big improvements were needed  getting rid of the tether and making the  disks suction not just with the outer  lip but also adding suction to  individual lamella  to get rid of the tether the researchers  installed two very small water pumps on  the robot  one pump bends the disc to position it  along a surface while the other controls  the lamellae  once the disk presses down on a surface  a tight seal is created from the  difference in pressure between the  compartment and environment as well as  the slight rotation of the lamellae to  make the robot latch even stronger fluid  is pumped from the chamber back to the  lamellae this further rotates the  lamellae and engages the spinules it  also reduces the pressure in the  compartment improving adhesion even more  by creating multiple seals the disc can  stick around even if some of the  compartments aren't firmly attached just  as the researchers observed in real life  remoras  the disc could latch on to a variety of  surfaces both in the air and underwater  from the curved smooth shell of a  mollusk to surfaces riddled with  microorganisms  the disc can stay attached to surfaces  for hours and even hitch a ride onto  moving objects  next the researchers channeled the  capabilities of a kingfisher  and added flight to their hitch-hiking  robot  the robot uses propellers to swim to the  surface then shoot itself out of the  water in less than half a second  outside the lab the robot proved it  could deal with different environments  throughout these excursions a camera on  board provided a view of the surrounding  environment while the robot can only  swim up to a depth of about 2 meters  before wireless communication cuts out  it can continue to record at greater  depths  in the future the researchers hope to  add other monitors on board such as a  sensor to detect when the disk is fully  attached or if there's a leak  someday this little bot might be diving  for your phone when you drop it off the  boardwalk or riding along on a whale's  journey  [Music]  [Music]
 

